Summary
=======

- Load existing TorchSig train, validation, and test datasets from their
  configured dataset-ID directories.
- Preserve support for the nested `<train dataset ID>/train`, `val`, and
  `test` layout used when generating datasets.
- Validate existing splits and report which path is missing or invalid.
- Avoid dataset generation and filesystem writes when reusing datasets with
  `overwrite=False`.

Motivation
==========

`prepare_torchsig_datasets()` previously assumed every split lived below the
training dataset ID. Published TorchSig datasets can instead give each split
its own dataset ID, such as `narrowband_impaired_train_all`,
`narrowband_impaired_val_all`, and `narrowband_impaired_test_all`. The old path
resolution attempted to create nested directories under the training dataset,
which failed for read-only dataset mounts.

Implementation
==============

The dataset preparation helper now detects an existing nested layout or an
existing dataset-ID-based layout before creating directories. Existing splits
are opened directly with `StaticTorchSigDataset` and the reader selected by
each split's configuration. Class names are recovered from `dataset_info.yaml`
when available, with the configuration metadata as a fallback.

If an existing layout is only partially present, or a split cannot be read,
the helper raises a descriptive split-specific exception. When no existing
layout is found, dataset creation continues to use the original nested layout.
Setting `overwrite=True` also retains the existing regeneration behavior.

Testing
=======

- Verify separate train, validation, and test dataset-ID directories are
  loaded without calling `DatasetCreator`.
- Verify existing nested split directories are still loaded directly.
- Verify partial existing layouts identify the missing split.
- Verify unreadable datasets produce descriptive errors.
- Run the complete dataset utility test module and formatting/lint checks.
